  "content": {
    "blocks": [
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "body": {
          "en": "When an equation cannot be solved exactly, sign changes locate roots and iteration refines them.",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "thm",
        "label": {
          "en": "▲ Reference — Finding roots",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "A sign change of \\( f \\) across \\( [a,b] \\) traps a root. Iteration \\( x_{n+1}=g(x_n) \\) converges when it settles; the Newton–Raphson step is \\( x_{n+1}=x_n-\\tfrac{f(x_n)}{f'(x_n)} \\).",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "cards",
        "items": [
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Sign change",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "root in \\( [a,b] \\)",
              "ar": null
            }
          },
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Iteration",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "\\( x_{n+1}=g(x_n) \\)",
              "ar": null
            }
          },
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Newton",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "\\( x_n-\\tfrac{f}{f'} \\)",
              "ar": null
            }
          },
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Converge",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "value settles",
              "ar": null
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "example",
        "label": {
          "en": "Worked example 10.A",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "<p>For \\( f(x)=x^{2}-2 \\), between which integers does a root lie?</p>\\[ 1\\ \\text{and}\\ 2,\\ \\text{since}\\ f(1)&lt;0&lt;f(2). \\]",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "example",
        "label": {
          "en": "Worked example — Newton-Raphson",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "<p>Use Newton-Raphson to find the root of \\(f(x)=x^3-5\\) near \\(x_0=1.7\\). Do one iteration.</p><p>\\(f(x)=x^3-5\\), \\(f'(x)=3x^2\\).</p>\\[x_1=x_0-\\frac{f(x_0)}{f'(x_0)}=1.7-\\frac{1.7^3-5}{3(1.7)^2}=1.7-\\frac{4.913-5}{8.67}=1.7-\\frac{-0.087}{8.67}\\approx1.710.\\]",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "practiceProblem",
        "question": {
          "en": "Show that \\(x^3+2x-6=0\\) has a root in \\([1,2]\\). Taking \\(x_0=1.5\\), perform one Newton-Raphson iteration.",
          "ar": null
        },
        "solution": {
          "en": "\\(f(1)=-3&lt;0\\), \\(f(2)=6&gt;0\\): sign change confirms root. \\(f(1.5)=1.5^3+3-6=-0.625\\). \\(f'(x)=3x^2+2\\Rightarrow f'(1.5)=8.75\\). \\(x_1=1.5-\\tfrac{-0.625}{8.75}\\approx1.571\\).",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "figure",
        "figure": {
          "type": "fn",
          "fns": [
            { "fn": "x^3 - 5", "label": "f(x) = x^3 - 5" }
          ],
          "points": [
            { "x": 1.71, "y": 0, "label": "Root ≈ 1.71" },
            { "x": 1.7, "y": -0.087, "label": "x₀ = 1.7" }
          ],
          "xrange": [0, 3],
          "yrange": [-6, 6]
        },
        "caption": {
          "en": "Newton-Raphson approximation to the root of f(x) = x^3 - 5 showing rapid convergence from initial estimate x₀ = 1.7.",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "warn",
        "label": {
          "en": "⚠️ Cambridge 9709 Traps — Continuity in Sign Change & Iteration Divergence",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "<p><strong>Sign Change Continuity Requirement:</strong> A change of sign \\(f(a)f(b) < 0\\) guarantees a root ONLY if \\(f(x)\\) is continuous on \\([a, b]\\). Functions with vertical asymptotes (such as \\(f(x) = \\tan x\\) or \\(f(x) = \\frac{1}{x}\\)) can change sign across a discontinuity without having any root. Cambridge always requires students to explicitly state that the function is continuous.</p><p><strong>Fixed-Point Convergence Condition:</strong> An iteration \\(x_{n+1} = g(x_n)\\) converges to a root \\(\\alpha\\) if and only if \\(|g'(\\alpha)| < 1\\). If \\(|g'(\\alpha)| > 1\\), the iteration will diverge or oscillate away from the root. If asked to explain why an iterative formula fails, evaluate \\(|g'(x)|\\) near the root.</p>",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "practiceProblem",
        "question": {
          "en": "The equation \\(x^3 - x - 1 = 0\\) has a root near \\(x = 1.3\\).\n(a) Show that the iterative formula \\(x_{n+1} = (x_n + 1)^{1/3}\\) converges by considering \\(g'(x)\\).\n(b) Starting with \\(x_1 = 1.3\\), calculate \\(x_2\\) and \\(x_3\\) to 4 decimal places.",
          "ar": null
        },
        "solution": {
          "en": "<p>(a) Let \\(g(x) = (x + 1)^{1/3}\\). Differentiate:</p>\\[g'(x) = \\frac{1}{3}(x + 1)^{-2/3} = \\frac{1}{3(x + 1)^{2/3}}\\]<p>Near the root \\(x \\approx 1.3\\):</p>\\[g'(1.3) = \\frac{1}{3(2.3)^{2/3}} \\approx \\frac{1}{3(1.742)} \\approx 0.191\\]<p>Since \\(|g'(x)| < 1\\), the iteration is guaranteed to converge rapidly.</p><p>(b) Carry out iterations:</p>\\[x_1 = 1.3\\]\\[x_2 = (1.3 + 1)^{1/3} = (2.3)^{1/3} \\approx 1.3200\\]\\[x_3 = (1.3200 + 1)^{1/3} = (2.3200)^{1/3} \\approx 1.3238\\]",
          "ar": null
        }
      }
    ]
  },
